---
/**
 * The precedents.
 *
 * Answers one question: why is it not crazy to think a constitution can
 * authorise its own destruction?
 *
 * Because three of them did, within twelve years of each other, and Gödel was
 * living in one of them at the time. That is the honest reason to take the
 * claim seriously, and it is also the reason to distrust our own method: the
 * three cases ran at three different depths and only the shallowest is visible
 * to anyone reading a constitution.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MarginNote from '../components/MarginNote.astro';
import Value from '../components/Value.astro';
import CollapseLayers from '../islands/CollapseLayers.astro';
import { fact } from '../lib/facts';
import { readSections, pick } from '../lib/content';

const [why] = pick(readSections('eli5.md'), 'why-anyone-thinks-this-is-possible');
const filter = fact('collapse.filter');
const rigidity = fact('articlev.rigidity');
const protection = fact('articlev.protection');
const gThreshold = fact('collapse.germany.threshold');
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="The precedents"
  description="Three constitutions died between the wars, each at a different depth: Germany 1933 through the amendment clause, Austria 1933 through a statute outside the constitution, Italy 1925 through ordinary law with the text untouched. They are kept as a filter on this project's own methods."
  section="precedents"
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="wide lede">
      <p class="kicker smallcaps">Why this is not a crazy question</p>
      <h1>Three constitutions died this way, and each died at a different depth.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        Gödel was not speculating about something that had never happened. He had
        watched it happen to his own country nine years earlier, and he emigrated
        because of it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-happened">
      <h2 id="what-happened">{why.title}</h2>
      <div class="prose" set:html={why.html} />
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="Canonical telling">
      <p>
        The narrative above is rendered from <code>eli5.md</code> at build time rather
        than retold here. There is one copy of it.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <section aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The three depths</h2>
      <p>
        The cases are usually told as one story about the 1930s. They are three
        different stories, and the difference is the part that matters to a project
        that proposes to search a constitution for a defect.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only Germany&#8217;s path went through the constitution. Austria&#8217;s ran
        through a law the constitution never mentions. Italy&#8217;s ran through
        ordinary statutes while the constitutional text sat unamended from beginning
        to end.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide">
      <CollapseLayers />
    </div>

    <section aria-labelledby="filter">
      <h2 id="filter">This is a test we set for ourselves, not a result</h2>
      <p>
        {filter.value} That is not a historical observation. It is a standing
        objection to the method this project is built on, and it is kept in front of
        us on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        {filter.statement} A technique that recovers Germany and misses the other two
        has not been validated; it has been fitted to the one case that happens to
        suit it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Italy is held out deliberately as the case where the method is
        <em>predicted to fail</em>. A version of this project that reported a finding
        there would have shown that its procedures generate results from any input,
        which is the specific failure everything here is built to avoid.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="The three analyses" facts={['collapse.germany', 'collapse.austria', 'collapse.italy']} />

    <section aria-labelledby="arithmetic">
      <h2 id="arithmetic">The German case has arithmetic in it that nobody quotes</h2>
      <p>
        Article&nbsp;76 is described everywhere as a two-thirds amendment rule. It was
        two rules. Two thirds of the statutory membership had to be present, and two
        thirds of those present had to assent, and those fractions compose.
      </p>
      <p class="pull">
        Two thirds of two thirds is {gThreshold.value}. About forty-four per cent.
      </p>
      <p>
        A clause whose entire purpose is to stop a majority from rewriting the
        constitution could be satisfied by a minority of the chamber. This needs no
        source beyond the clause and multiplication, and it falls straight out of the
        same threshold procedure that produces the American numbers on
        <a href={`${base}/text/`}>the text page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The actual vote was nowhere near that minimum, and the honest reading of that
        matters. The usual telling is that excluding the eighty-one Communist deputies
        is what carried the Enabling Act. The Bundestag&#8217;s own account says the
        opposite.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote
      head="Article 76"
      facts={['collapse.germany.threshold', 'collapse.germany.votes']}
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="not-america" class="caveat">
      <h2 id="not-america">What this does <em>not</em> show about the United States</h2>
      <p>
        The comparison is the first thing a reader will reach for and it comes out the
        other way. {rigidity.statement}
      </p>
      <p>
        {rigidity.value} Anyone using the number
        <Value id="threshold.house.propose" bare /> to argue that the 1947 Constitution
        was as exposed as Weimar&#8217;s is misusing it, and this page says so before
        that argument can be built on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The narrow statement that survives is about <em>where</em> the protection sits.
        {protection.value}
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="The narrow statement" facts={['articlev.rigidity', 'articlev.protection']} />

    <nav class="wide next" aria-label="Continue">
      <p class="smallcaps">Next</p>
      <p>
        Two ways to look for a defect. <a href={`${base}/text/`}>Read the words</a>, or
        <a href={`${base}/machine/`}>treat the document as a machine</a>.
      </p>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* A pulled sentence, used once per page at most, and only where the sentence
     is the finding rather than a summary of it. */
  .pull {
    font-family: var(--font-document);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin: var(--space-md) 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--graphic-strong);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /* The section that argues against the obvious reading of this page. It is
     set apart because a reader skimming for the exciting number needs to hit
     the correction on the way past, not after they have left. */
  .caveat {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    padding: var(--space-md) 0 var(--space-sm);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .next {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-sm);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .next .smallcaps {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
</style>
